LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Two identity systems, one architecture decision", "13", "<p>Both trading tracks met identity at their gates — Selling reading it out, Procurement writing it in. This module is identity's own curriculum: the warehouse lives with these records every day, and the warehouse is where they earn their keep or rot.</p><p><b>The two systems, distinguished precisely.</b> A <b>serial number</b> makes each UNIT an individual: one serial, one physical thing, one biography — received here, sits there, delivered to them, returned, serviced. A serial exists in exactly one place at a time (or one state: delivered, in stock, returned), which is what makes it a biography rather than a tally. A <b>batch</b> makes units MEMBERS of a lot: one batch, many units, one shared story — the same production run, the same expiry, the same recall fate. A batch, unlike a serial, lives in many places at once: B-1147 can hold 60 cartons at Central, 25 at Ibadan, 5 in transit, and the batch's position is a per-warehouse read. Serial answers <i>which one?</i>; batch answers <i>which lot, and how much of it, where?</i></p><p><b>What each enables — the coverage bought.</b> Serials buy: warranty and service anchoring (the chiller's plate number opening its file), theft and swap detection (the unit that returns is provably the unit that left), unit-level custody in transfers and audits. Batches buy: expiry management and FEFO (Chapter 4), the recall in both directions (Chapter 5), and quality attribution by production run. Neither is free: every gate slows by the capture minutes, every movement carries the bookkeeping, and the flags are why — which makes the flag decision an ARCHITECTURE decision, not a preference.</p><p><b>The flag decision, revisited as architecture.</b> Buying 2 set the flags at item creation; this chapter is the reasoning at full depth. Serialize where the unit is individually valuable, serviceable, or fraud-attractive (equipment, devices, high-value electronics); batch where goods expire, are regulated, or fail by production run (pharma, food, cosmetics — for your clients, most of the shelf); NEITHER where the item is cheap, stable, and interchangeable (the biro needs no biography). The costs of getting it wrong are asymmetric and both real: flags ON without operational need is a permanent tax on every gate for coverage nobody reads (Chapter 9's identity theatre); flags OFF where regulation or service needs them is a retrofit — and the retrofit is genuinely painful: existing unidentified stock cannot retroactively acquire provenance, so the transition is a managed cutover (existing stock counted into identified form with honest unknowns marked), not a checkbox. Decide at item birth with the ten-year view; the item architecture chapter said it, and now you can see everything it was protecting.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The serial lifecycle: a biography in states", "13", "<p>A serialized unit's record is a small dossier the system maintains for life. Reading and trusting that dossier is the skill; this chapter walks the life.</p><p><b>Birth.</b> The serial is born on a Purchase Receipt (Procurement 6's most consequential data entry): captured from the physical unit — the plate, the engraving, the manufacturer's label — never from the packing list. From that entry the dossier opens: item, supplier, purchase document, receipt date, cost. Everything after appends.</p><p><b>The states of a life.</b> At any moment the serial is in exactly one state, and the state answers the operational question: <b>in stock</b> — at a named warehouse (the serial's location is a fact, not a search); <b>in transit</b> — riding a two-step transfer, the room answering for it by name; <b>delivered</b> — with a named customer via a named Delivery Note (the warranty clock's anchor, the proof in any dispute about what was supplied when); <b>returned</b> — back through the return documents, the biography recording the round trip and the triage verdict (restock, quarantine, repair path); and terminal states — written off with its governance, or expired from the system's concern by policy. The dossier is the union of the states: any serial, any moment, one screen answers where it is, where it has been, and by which documents.</p><p><b>Movements by name.</b> Serialized stock never moves as a quantity — every movement names its serials: the transfer lists which units ride the truck (and the receiving branch confirms those units arrived — the swap-en-route catch), the delivery lists which units the customer got (the exact-unit dispute-ender), the write-off names its dead. The bookkeeping cost is real and the purchase is precision: quantity-level custody says three chillers left; serial-level custody says WHICH three, and only one of those statements survives contact with a warranty claim, a theft investigation, or an insurance file.</p><p><b>The service anchor.</b> For equipment trade, the serial is where after-sales lives: the service history appended per unit, the warranty window computed from the delivery's date, the repeat-failure pattern visible per unit and per batch-of-units (three failures on units from one receipt is a supplier conversation with evidence). The consultant selling serialization to a client sells THIS — not compliance, but the end of every argument that begins <i>the one we bought from you</i>: the dossier either shows that unit, that date, that document — or shows the unit was never yours, which ends a different and more expensive argument (the counterfeit warranty claim, the unit bought elsewhere seeking your service). The impossible return from Selling 5 is this chapter's machinery doing its outbound job.</p><p><b>Reading the dossier as diagnosis.</b> Like the stock ledger it lives inside, the serial's history is the first stop when a unit's story is disputed: the state that surprises decomposes into its documents. A serial in stock that cannot be found is a count matter (module 4); a serial delivered that the customer denies is a POD matter (the DN's evidence); a serial that appears twice is Chapter 6's ghost hunt. The dossier does not argue; it cites.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The batch lifecycle: the lot across the network", "13", "<p>A batch is a population, and managing it is demography: how much of lot B-1147 exists, where, in what states, expiring when. The mechanics differ from serials in exactly the ways that matter operationally.</p><p><b>Birth and the shared facts.</b> The batch is born at receipt — lot number and <b>expiry</b> keyed from the physical cartons (the entry FEFO and the recall both stand on). Its shared facts bind every member unit: one expiry, one supplier provenance (via the receipt), one quality fate. Multiple receipts can feed one batch (the same lot arriving in two drops — the same lot number keys to the same batch record) and one item holds many live batches simultaneously — the normal state of a pharmacy shelf.</p><p><b>The per-warehouse position.</b> Unlike a serial, a batch disperses: its balance is a GRID — batch × warehouse — and the batch-wise stock read answers the demographic questions: B-1147 holds 60 at Central, 25 at Ibadan, 5 in transit; B-1152, younger, holds 180 at Central untouched. Movements draw down the batch at the source and build it at the target — the batch splits across rooms without losing identity, and every split is ledgered. The read matters daily (which batch does Ibadan actually hold?) and existentially in the recall (Chapter 5's first question is exactly this grid).</p><p><b>Batches do not merge.</b> The rule with no exceptions: two lots are two lots forever. The temptation arrives at the shelf (the half-carton of B-1147 topped up from B-1152's carton — one bin, mixed physically) and at the desk (the clerk recording the mixed shelf as one batch for tidiness). Physical mixing may be a shelf reality the firm manages (or prohibits — pharma practice generally prohibits it precisely for this reason); LEDGER mixing is never acceptable: the recorded quantities stay per-batch, picked per-batch, sold per-batch — because a merged record cannot be unmerged the day NAFDAC names one of the two lots, and the merged shelf's whole quantity inherits the recall. Keep lots distinct in the record, and where the physical shelf must hold two, the pick discipline (Chapter 4's FEFO plus batch selection at the till) keeps the record true.</p><p><b>Batch attributes beyond expiry.</b> The batch record carries what the lot shares: manufacturing date where captured, the supplier's own lot references (the cross-walk NAFDAC correspondence uses), and quality notes appended by inspections or incidents — the batch that drew a customer complaint carries the note, and the second complaint against the same lot is a pattern the record surfaces (quarantine the remainder pending review: the fork, batch-triggered).</p><p><b>The batch as cost carrier.</b> Where valuation runs at batch granularity, each lot carries its own cost story (the January import's landed rate vs March's), and margin reads per batch become possible — the operational payoff being honest answers to <i>are we making money on the old stock we discounted?</i> Module 5 owns valuation's mechanics; plant the connection: identity granularity and cost granularity are the same decision seen from two chairs.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Expiry operations: the horizon, FEFO & the markdown", "14", "<p>For your pharmacy, food, and FMCG clients, expiry is the identity system's daily workout — and the difference between a managed horizon and an annual write-off shock is entirely operational discipline. This chapter is that discipline.</p><p><b>The horizon read.</b> The standing report: batch balances by days-to-expiry — expired (should be zero in stock, quarantined pending disposal), 0-30 days, 31-60, 61-90, beyond. Read weekly at minimum, per warehouse (the network's expiry is not one number: Central's fresh pallets and Ibadan's aging shelf are different problems), and read as a WORKLIST: each near bucket's lines carry a decision owner. The horizon's whole purpose is converting expiry from an event that happens TO the firm into a pipeline the firm works.</p><p><b>FEFO in operation.</b> First-Expired-First-Out: picks and sales draw the earliest-expiring batch first. The system's role is suggestion and enforcement at the pick/sale moment (the batch offered first is the earliest-dated with stock at that warehouse); the human role is compliance and honest exception — the override exists (the customer who needs the longest dating for their own shelf life gets it; an export order's minimum-dating clause binds), and the override is LOGGED, because habitual override is how FEFO dies quietly: the pickers grabbing the front row, the fresh batch bleeding out while the old one ages to death at the back. The FEFO-compliance read (overrides by user and reason, monthly) is the discipline's heartbeat — near-total compliance with occasional explained exceptions is health; drift is the write-off line's future.</p><p><b>The markdown ladder.</b> Between fresh and dead sits the decision zone, and the firm's SHORT-DATING POLICY is a ladder written in advance: at N days remaining, flag for push (front-of-shelf, staff awareness); at M days, markdown per the discount authority (Selling 3's rules — the policy IS a pricing rule, pre-approved, not a per-carton negotiation); at the floor, the channels of last honest resort where the trade allows (the bulk-to-institutional sale with dating disclosed); past saleable, quarantine for disposal. Every rung is cheaper than the rung below it, which is the whole argument for working the horizon early: the ₦9,600 carton sold at ₦8,200 in month one of the window beats the ₦9,600 write-off in month three, every time, and the ladder makes the arithmetic routine instead of painful.</p><p><b>The disposal endgame.</b> Expired regulated goods exit through governance, not the bin: quarantined on expiry (out of sellable truth the day the date passes — the horizon read's expired bucket must be empty of SELLABLE stock, always), written off with Inv 2's signatures and batch-level identity, and physically destroyed per the compliance regime — pharma disposal is witnessed, documented, and filed, with the write-off document part of the evidence chain, not a substitute for it. The batch biography ends: born at receipt, lived across the grid, died with paperwork.</p><p><b>The upstream feedback.</b> Expiry losses are a message, and the horizon's trend is the envelope: chronic short-dating losses on a line indict buying quantities (Procurement 2-3's planning fields — buying past the sell-through), gate acceptance (Procurement 6's minimum-dating rule too loose), or velocity assumptions (the item slowed and the buying never heard). The monthly expiry-loss read, by item and cause, is the warehouse telling procurement the truth — and this track giving the earlier tracks their feedback loop.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The recall run-book", "15", "<p>The recall is the identity system's final exam, and it is taken under time pressure with a regulator watching. This chapter turns the drill both earlier tracks previewed into a RUN-BOOK — the written procedure a competent team executes in hours, not weeks.</p><p><b>The trigger.</b> NAFDAC (or the manufacturer) names a batch. The clock starts. The run-book's steps, in order, each with its instrument:</p><ul><li><b>Step 1 — freeze the batch (minutes).</b> The named lot is blocked from sale and movement network-wide: batch-level hold, tills and pickers warned, the batch's per-warehouse grid (Chapter 3) pulled as the working document. Nothing else proceeds until nothing of the lot can move.</li><li><b>Step 2 — the inbound trace (minutes).</b> The receipt(s) that brought the batch: supplier, PO, dates, quantities, the supplier's own lot references and documentation from the shipment file (Procurement 1 and 6 built this shelf; today it is opened). This is the packet the NAFDAC and manufacturer correspondence runs on.</li><li><b>Step 3 — the holdings sweep (within the hour).</b> Current stock of the lot, per warehouse INCLUDING the logical rooms — the transit balance, the quarantine bay, the repacker's supplier-side room (the repack mapping from Inv 2 traces which produced strips carry the lot). Physical verification against the grid: the counted lot moved to quarantine, every warehouse confirming.</li><li><b>Step 4 — the outbound trace (within hours).</b> Every Delivery Note and sale carrying the batch: customers, branches, quantities, dates — Selling 5's drill executed from the warehouse chair. The output is the CONTACT LIST: who must be told, what they received, when.</li><li><b>Step 5 — customer contact & retrieval.</b> Per the firm's recall policy and the regulator's instruction: notifications out (documented), returns in through the return machinery with batch identity confirmed at the gate (what comes back is provably the lot), retrieved stock into quarantine joining the sweep.</li><li><b>Step 6 — the reconciliation.</b> The closing arithmetic, and the step that separates a defensible recall from a hopeful one: <b>received − sold − held − returned − written-off = zero</b>, per warehouse, documented. Every unit of the lot accounted for by a ledger line; the residual, if any, named and hunted. This page is what the regulator, the manufacturer, and your own counsel each want, and the identity disciplines of two tracks exist so this page can be produced.</li></ul><p><b>The run-book as artifact.</b> Written, owned, and DRILLED: the annual mock recall (pick a batch, run the book, time it) is cheap insurance that finds the gaps on a Tuesday instead of during the real letter — the transit room someone forgot to sweep, the branch that receives batch-blind, the repack mapping nobody spot-checked. Target times are set honestly per the firm (hours for a disciplined single-region network) and the mock's timing is the identity system's health certificate. A firm that can produce the reconciliation page in four hours is a firm whose gates, movements, and records all held; every failure mode in this module's Chapter 9 shows up first as a mock-recall gap.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Identity hygiene: ghosts, orphans & the audits", "13", "<p>Identity records drift from reality exactly as quantities do, and the drift has its own bestiary. This chapter names the creatures and installs the audits that hunt them.</p><p><b>The bestiary.</b> <b>Ghost serials</b>: recorded units that do not physically exist — born from packing-list capture (the list said five, four arrived), duplicate keying, or receipts of units later swapped. A ghost sits in stock forever, inflates the count expectation, and surfaces as the impossible situation (the unit sold that was never here, the audit that cannot find it). <b>Orphan batches</b>: quantity in a batch the physical shelf cannot show, or shelf stock whose batch the record cannot name — born from batch-blind movements (the transfer that carried quantity but not lot), lazy till picks (sold from B-1147 on screen, handed from B-1152 by hand), and capture skipped at busy gates. Orphans are the recall's nightmare: the reconciliation that will not zero. <b>The double life</b>: one physical unit under two records (re-keyed on a correction that never voided the original) — the audit finds it as a serial in two states at once.</p><p><b>The serial audit.</b> Periodic, warehouse by warehouse: the system's in-stock serial list against a physical scan or sight of the units. Three outcomes per line: matched (the healthy majority), recorded-not-found (ghost candidates — investigated before written off: mis-shelved? in an unrecorded state? actually gone, which is a custody matter for module 4's machinery), found-not-recorded (the reverse ghost — a unit with no record, entered with Inv 2's found-stock discipline and a hard look at which gate leaked). Cadence follows value: high-value serial stock quarterly; the audit rides the counting programme module 4 builds rather than duplicating it.</p><p><b>The batch integrity read.</b> The batch equivalent, run as arithmetic before it is run as legwork: per item, the sum of batch balances must equal the bin's actual (a gap means quantity moving batch-blind somewhere — find the leaking document type); expired batches must show zero sellable (the horizon's standing rule); and the spot-check at counts — shelf cartons' printed lots against the recorded batch mix for that warehouse — catches the till-pick drift (screen says B-1147, hand said B-1152) before it compounds. Where drift is found, the correction is a documented batch adjustment with a reason, and the FIX is upstream: the till discipline retrained, the gate's capture tightened, the transfer receiving made batch-level (Inv 2's rule, now with its enforcement audit).</p><p><b>Overrides and exceptions, read monthly.</b> The identity system's pressure valves — FEFO overrides, manual batch selections, serial substitutions at delivery — are each legitimate singly and diagnostic in aggregate: the monthly exceptions read by user, item, and reason shows where the discipline bends, and chronic bending at one till, one gate, or one item is a training or design conversation the read triggers. The principle across all of it is module 1's, applied to identity: <b>the records are testimony, and the audits are how testimony keeps its credibility</b> — cheap, calendared, and boring when everything is fine, which is precisely the state they exist to certify.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Identity in the daily flow", "13", "<p>Identity is not a crisis feature; it is a daily habit, and this chapter walks it through the ordinary day so the crisis chapters stay theoretical.</p><p><b>At the till and the counter.</b> Batch-tracked sales pick a batch at the moment of sale — FEFO offering the earliest, the pick confirmed against the physical carton in hand (the two-second glance at the printed lot that keeps the record true). The friction is real and small; the design answers keep it small: barcodes carrying batch where the trade supports them, the shelf discipline that FEFO's physical layout mirrors the system's suggestion (earliest at the front — the stocker enforces FEFO before the till ever asks), and the till trained that the batch line is custody, not ceremony. Serial sales are rarer and slower by nature (the unit's number confirmed at handover) and the slowness is the product: the customer watches their unit's identity recorded, which is the warranty's first document.</p><p><b>In transfers.</b> Inv 2 gave the rule; identity gives it teeth: dispatches list the batches and serials riding; the receiving branch confirms IDENTITY, not just quantity — 20 cartons arrived, but WHICH lots? The batch-level receipt is minutes and it is the only defence against the swap-en-route (good dating dispatched, short dating arrived, the difference sold out the back — a real pattern, and batch-blind receiving is what makes it invisible). The transit shortfall protocol inherits identity too: what is missing is named by lot, which scopes the loss (and the recall exposure, if the lot ever matters) precisely.</p><p><b>In returns.</b> The identity match is the gate's first question: the returning serial is checked against its delivery (the impossible-return catch — the unit we never sold, the unit sold elsewhere seeking our refund); the returning batch is confirmed against what that customer received (the credit claimed on goods from another source). Matches proceed to triage with biography intact; mismatches become a different conversation, held politely and from documents. The return desk with identity discipline is a fraud filter; without it, a laundry.</p><p><b>In repack and internal movements.</b> The mapping rule from Inv 2, now as daily habit: consumed batches to produced outputs recorded per entry, spot-checked per Chapter 6; write-offs and issues naming their lots always (the biography's ending is a ledger line, not a shrug); and quarantine's contents batch-labelled physically and in record — the bay the recall sweeps first should be the bay that answers fastest.</p><p><b>The habit's economics, told honestly.</b> Identity discipline costs seconds at every gate, till, and truck — a real, recurring, visible cost — and pays in events that are rare, invisible when prevented, and catastrophic when unprevented. Teams do not sustain disciplines they experience as pointless taxation, so the consultant's implementation job is making the payoff visible on ordinary days: the monthly identity page (audit results, exceptions read, FEFO compliance, expiry losses trending down) shown to the team that earned it; the mock recall's time posted like a fire-drill record; the war stories (the swap caught, the impossible return refused) told. The firms whose identity systems still work in year three are the firms where the team can say what the seconds buy.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: the letter arrives at HealthTrade", "16", "<p>The module's threads converge on one Tuesday morning: a NAFDAC letter names <b>batch B-1147</b> of PARA-500 — a manufacturer-level quality recall, all stock to be quarantined and accounted. The run-book comes off the shelf. Clock: 08:40.</p><p><b>08:40-08:55 — freeze (Ch. 5 step 1).</b> Batch hold applied network-wide; tills at all three sites see the block; the working grid pulled: B-1147 shows 34 cartons at Central, 12 at Ibadan, 0 at Abeokuta (their stock was always B-1152), 0 in transit — and, via the repack mapping, 88 retail strips at Central produced from the lot (Inv 2's entry cited by line). The physical freeze is confirmed by each storekeeper against the grid.</p><p><b>08:55-09:20 — inbound packet (step 2).</b> The receipt that bore B-1147 (Emzor drop one — Procurement 6's case study, now evidence): PO, receipt, quantities, the expiry as captured, Emzor's own lot documentation from the shipment file. The packet goes to the MD for the NAFDAC and Emzor correspondence — twenty-five minutes from letter to a documented inbound story.</p><p><b>09:20-10:30 — holdings verified, outbound traced (steps 3-4).</b> Physical counts confirm the grid (34 + 12 + 88 strips), all moved to quarantine bays, batch-labelled. The outbound trace runs: every DN and sale carrying B-1147 — 118 cartons across six weeks: 74 to four wholesale customers (named, quantified, dated), 44 across branch retail (aggregate, per the trade's nature and the regulator's tiering — retail-level recall handled by point-of-sale notice per the policy). The contact list is real by 10:30.</p><p><b>10:30-12:40 — contact & retrieval begin (step 5).</b> The four wholesale customers notified with their exact quantities and dates (documented); two confirm stock on hand for uplift — the returns will arrive through the gate with batch identity checked (what comes back must BE B-1147). Point-of-sale notices posted per policy. NAFDAC's acknowledgment records the response began same-morning.</p><p><b>12:40 — the reconciliation page (step 6).</b> Received 180 − sold 118 − held 46 (34+12) − repacked-held 16 (the strip equivalent) − written-off 0 = <b>zero unaccounted</b>, per warehouse, every figure a ledger line. Four hours from letter to a page the regulator, the manufacturer, and counsel can each hold. The mock drill run last quarter (3h 50m) predicted the time within minutes — and had already fixed the one gap it found (Ibadan's receiving was confirming quantity but not lot; retrained then, decisive today).</p><p><b>The quiet epilogues (Ch. 4, 6).</b> The same week's serial audit finds one ghost: a chiller serial recorded from a setup-era packing list, never physically present — investigated, written off with its story, and the capture-from-the-unit rule re-taught with a live exhibit. And the expiry horizon, doing its undramatic weekly work, flags B-1152's Ibadan balance entering the 90-day window with velocity too low to clear it: the markdown ladder's first rung (push and front-shelf) executes, and six weeks later the balance clears at full-to-first-rung prices — the write-off that never happened, invisible precisely because the horizon was read. The identity system's year: one crisis passed in four hours, one ghost exorcised, one markdown that saved ₦300k of quiet money — the seconds at the gates, priced.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the third law", "13", "<p>The identity layer's scar tissue — and every disease here shares one anatomy: coverage paid for at the gates and destroyed by a habit. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the recall that cannot run.</b> Symptom: the letter arrives; the batch's whereabouts are a research project. Disease: capture skipped or faked at the gates (the identity theatre — flags on, discipline off), movements batch-blind, the grid fictional. Fix: painful reconstruction once (supplier documents against sales records — weeks, in the newspapers' shadow), then the gate disciplines with their audits, and the mock recall as the standing certificate that it cannot happen again.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — FEFO dead by a thousand overrides.</b> Symptom: expiry write-offs climbing while the FEFO flag sits proudly on. Disease: habitual override (Ch. 4) — the front-row grab, the unlogged exception, the fresh batch bleeding out first. Fix: the compliance read by user and reason, the shelf layout that makes FEFO physical, and the ladder worked early so the horizon never becomes a cliff.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — ghosts in the serial file.</b> Symptom: in-stock serials nobody can produce; impossible sales; audits that will not close. Disease: packing-list capture and duplicate keying (Ch. 2, 6). Fix: capture from the unit as gate law, the periodic serial audit with its three-outcome triage, and ghosts exorcised by documented write-off with the leak that born them named and fixed.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the batch-blind branch.</b> Symptom: branch batch records diverge from shelves; the swap-en-route invisible; recall sweeps stall at one site. Disease: quantity-only receiving at transfers (Ch. 7) — identity dying at the network's joints. Fix: batch-level receipt as the transfer gate's rule, the integrity arithmetic (batch sums vs bin actuals) per warehouse monthly, and the mock recall timing each site separately so the weak joint names itself.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the merged lot.</b> Symptom: one recorded batch, two physical lots; a recall that must take everything. Disease: ledger mixing for tidiness (Ch. 3). Fix: lots distinct in record without exception, the physical mixing policy decided deliberately (and for pharma, usually prohibited), and the spot-check that compares printed cartons against the recorded mix.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — identity as decoration.</b> Symptom: the flags are on, the reports unread, the audits never run, the team cannot say what the seconds buy. Disease: coverage without operation — cost with no product (Ch. 1, 7). Fix: either OPERATE it (the monthly identity page, the calendar rhythms, the drill) or, for items where the honest answer is that nobody will ever read it, revisit the architecture decision consciously — a flag removed deliberately beats a discipline performed falsely.</p><p><b>The third law.</b> Module 1: the tree is the map of custody. Module 2: stock moves only by documents. This module: <b>identity is operational, not decorative — a serial or batch captured but never read is cost without coverage, and a discipline read daily makes the crisis drill just Tuesday.</b> Capture from the goods, move by name, receive by lot, pick by expiry, audit on the calendar, and drill the run-book — and the letter, when it comes, is four hours of executing a procedure instead of four weeks of archaeology.</p>"),
]

